WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.670
Surplus &amp; elasticity

2
00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:09.390
Write the midpoint elasticity formula.

3
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:18.950
ε = [(Q₂−Q₁) ÷ ((Q₁+Q₂)/2)] ÷ [(P₂−P₁) ÷ ((P₁+P₂)/2)]. The only elasticity formula you need.

4
00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:26.460
Price $4→$6, quantity 100→80. Elastic or inelastic?

5
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:39.830
%ΔQ = −20/90 = −22.2%; %ΔP = 2/5 = 40%; ε = −0.56 → inelastic.

6
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:44.060
Which way does revenue move?

7
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:52.270
Inelastic: revenue moves with price. Elastic: revenue moves against it. Unit elastic: revenue unchanged and at its maximum.

8
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:56.490
Why is elasticity not slope?

9
00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:07.640
A straight-line demand curve has one constant slope and a changing elasticity — elastic on the upper half, unit elastic at the midpoint, inelastic on the lower half. Revenue peaks at that midpoint.

10
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.980
What makes demand more elastic?

11
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:20.950
More close substitutes, a luxury rather than a necessity, a large share of the budget, a long time to adjust, and a narrowly defined market.

12
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:25.590
Consumer surplus — the two ways to find it?

13
00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:36.910
From a table, subtract price from each unit’s WTP and add the positives. From a graph, the area below demand and above price out to Q traded — usually ½ × base × height.

14
00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:42.830
Cross-price and income elasticity — what does the sign say?

15
00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:51.690
Cross-price positive = substitutes, negative = complements. Income positive = normal good, negative = inferior good.

16
00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:55.570
“Bang for your buck” condition?

17
00:01:55.570 --> 00:02:03.480
You are optimizing when MBx/Px = MBy/Py. If one side is bigger, buy more of that good.

18
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.000
Surplus &amp; elasticity — 8 cards
